  "notable": {
   "losses": {
    "title": "Drones lost",
    "html": "<p>Every loss was a pair. BBCC's were drones colliding at the tightest spread the dial allows (d_ref 0.4), the failure the damper was added for: it slowed the slow closings, not the fast ones that a building's shield or a hard stop forced. BBAA's one pair, at d_ref 1.0, began with a drone pinned on a street-light pole in a goal patch, a collider on the Default layer that the force law cannot see; another drone then ran into it.</p>"
   },
   "panorama": {
    "title": "The stitched panorama",
    "html": "<p>The panorama replaced the three front feeds for only a small part of each swarm run: its quality gate hid it whenever the views did not overlap well enough.</p>"
   }
  },
  "reading": "<p>The two pilots disagree: the swarm was quicker in total for BBAA and slower for BBCC, and each was quicker in the condition flown second.</p><p>That makes this series a measure of practice as much as of the swarm. The one phase both pilots agree on is the way between goals, where the swarm was quicker for both; at the goals BBAA's swarm took about half the single drone's time and BBCC's about twice. BBCC flew the swarm at the tightest spread and lost eight drones in its two swarm runs, while BBAA kept the spread near 1.0. With two pilots and two runs each, no ratio here could reach p 0.05 however one-sided.</p>",
  "data_notes": [
   "Practice was flown separately and not recorded: every run here counts.",
   "Answers were pressed on the RC, so the total time ends at the last press; the presses' outcomes were not keyed, so the identification card shows presses, not correct answers.",
   "The editor log of that day is not in the series folder, so the losses carry no recorded reason here; the log read on 1 October named every one a drone-drone collision (one pair after a street-light pole).",
   "The task is timed from the start line, found in the drone logs; the series itself was timed from Play."
  ],
  "summary_text": "Two pilots on 1 October, two runs in each condition: BBAA flew the single drone first, BBCC the swarm. The city was DiamondCityWorld, the tile city laid out eight by eight with its rows staggered and four diamond-shaped parks, each a superblock of four tiles. Practice was flown separately and not recorded, so every run here counts. The pilot pressed the RC's button at each identify; the outcomes were not keyed."
